--TEST--
`Core\Request`'s refusal names the member that was read exactly, so the singular header reader and the plural one are told apart by their own messages -- ADR 0012 § 7, ADR 0020 § 1, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement asked the other way round: every member shares one refusal *rule*,
// and each has to name itself while stating it. `header` and `headers` are the
// pair where that is worth pinning -- one is the other's name plus a letter, so
// a reader that passed its sibling's name to the shared refusal would produce a
// sentence that is true, well-formed, and about the wrong member.
try {
    Core\Request::header("content-type");
} catch (LogicError $one) {
    echo $one->message, "\n";
}
try {
    Core\Request::headers();
} catch (LogicError $all) {
    echo $all->message, "\n";
}

// And a program continues past both: the refusal is a rung of ADR 0020's ladder
// rather than a fatal, so a handler standing on two reads of one class reaches
// the line after them.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Request::header(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
Core\Request::headers(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
still running
